sections.body:
Recall from the toolchain concept that a package is roughly one directory of `.go` files sharing a `package` declaration. Within a single module, other packages are imported by their full path relative to the module root:

```go
// go.mod: module example.com/shop

// file: shop/inventory/inventory.go
package inventory

func NewTracker() *Tracker { ... }

// file: shop/main.go
package main

import (
	"example.com/shop/inventory"
)

func main() {
	tracker := inventory.NewTracker()
}
```

Note the calling convention: you reference an imported package's exported identifiers with the package name as a prefix -- `inventory.NewTracker()`, not just `NewTracker()` -- which makes every call site immediately clear about where a given function actually comes from, without needing to scroll up to check an import list the way you sometimes do in languages that allow unqualified imports of individual names. This is a small thing that pays off constantly when reading unfamiliar code: you can always tell, at the call site itself, which package a name belongs to.

The Go standard library has a distinctive philosophy that's worth understanding explicitly, because it shapes how idiomatic Go code -- both in the standard library and in the broader ecosystem -- tends to be designed: **favor small, focused interfaces with one or two methods over large abstract base classes or 'do everything' frameworks.**

The canonical example is `io.Reader` and `io.Writer`:

```go
type Reader interface {
	Read(p []byte) (n int, err error)
}

type Writer interface {
	Write(p []byte) (n int, err error)
}
```

That's the *entire* definition of `io.Reader` -- one method. And yet an enormous amount of the standard library, and of real-world Go code, is built on top of just this one interface: files (`os.File`), network connections (`net.Conn`), in-memory buffers (`bytes.Buffer`, `strings.Reader`), HTTP request/response bodies, gzip decompressors, and countless others all implement `Read(p []byte) (n int, err error)` -- which means a function written to accept an `io.Reader` parameter automatically works with *any* of them, present or future, without that function's author needing to have anticipated every concrete type in advance, and without the concrete type's author needing to explicitly declare 'I implement io.Reader' anywhere (Go's interfaces are satisfied implicitly, purely by having the right method signature -- there's no `implements` keyword at all).

`io.Copy(dst io.Writer, src io.Reader) (int64, error)` is the payoff: one small function, written once against two one-method interfaces, that correctly copies bytes from *any* readable source to *any* writable destination -- a file to a network connection, an HTTP response body to a buffer, standard input to a compressor -- with zero special-casing for any of them. Compare this to a framework built around a large base class with a dozen methods you must inherit and override many of just to plug in a single new implementation: small interfaces mean any type that already happens to have the right one or two methods can be used somewhere that never even knew that type existed at design time.

